{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2489 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "714b463c530d5ca88d3f542bef31ca3164f79b479262a6284f661d9f80d6cca6" :edb/db-before-t 2488 :edb/db-after-t 2489 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535801 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:30:44.030Z" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161732 1100 "Consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/cc0456884aa670c8c7f86387574a187f151f4ccc4e4275671425af1aa61812e8.png) By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/acf8b72ee7b927e5e82570c655a671a62b595a29c8191a64ad22ac98af8cf7a4.png)\n\n- $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161732 1100 "Consider the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n1. $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$" 13194139535801 false] [17592186161732 1145 "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/29545f79f3b34ca5395f98fa8c8c81e289482e866c3ad8c576aeb7b9fe8b8749.png)\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={2}^{2}-3\\cdot {7}^{2}+5\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =4-147+70 \\\\  & =-73<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =110>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={7}^{2}+8\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =49+112 \\\\  & =161>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161732 1145 "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n$R(x)|= 2^{2} - 3 \\cdot 7^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 4 - 147 + 70; =-73 < 0$.\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$S(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = 110 > 0$.\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$T(x)|= 7^{2} + 8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 49 + 112; = 161 > 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535801 false] [17592186161733 1100 "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/d16c12fa9086e956b3b0516d42b40aaa121e3b40bd25a72c3d05d436ba410440.png) Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161733 1100 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 false] [17592186161733 1145 "Given a quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwe have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A$, we obtain\n$\\mid A - λI \\mid |= 0;\\begin{vmatrix}-4 - λ & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix}|= 0; (-4 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 25|= 0; λ^{2} + 8λ - 9|= 0; (λ - 1)(λ + 9)|= 0; λ|= 1,-9$\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535801 false] [17592186161733 1145 "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161734 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/d81ce57017adec7daf2556322259f32ce4261e646dc8f1a48632beb085d9a9cb.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161734 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 false] [17592186161734 1145 "Let $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6$. Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get\n$det(A)|=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-5)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)(-2) + (5)(-7) + 12; =-11$.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n$Δ_{1}|= a_{11} =-6 < 0; Δ_{2}|= \\mid - 6\\begin{vmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 37 > 0; Δ_{3}|= \\mid A|=-11 < 0$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$, and the matrix $A$, are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535801 false] [17592186161734 1145 "Let $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i <          0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6.$ Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-5)|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)(-2)+(5)(-7)+12 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|=37>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-11<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x},$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186161940 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366925 13194139535801 true] [17592186161940 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366960 13194139535801 true] [17592186161940 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366967 13194139535801 true] [17592186161940 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366974 13194139535801 true] [17592186161941 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366932 13194139535801 true] [17592186161941 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366939 13194139535801 true] [17592186161941 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366946 13194139535801 true] [17592186161941 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366981 13194139535801 true] [17592186161942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366953 13194139535801 true] [17592186161942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366988 13194139535801 true] [17592186161942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366995 13194139535801 true] [17592186161942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367002 13194139535801 true] [17592186162560 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535801 true] [17592186162560 1100 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" 13194139535801 true] [17592186162560 1100 "Consider the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n1. $R(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{1}x_{2}$\n2. $S(x) = x^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$\n3. $T(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2}$" 13194139535801 false] [17592186162560 1145 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186162563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535801 true] [17592186162563 1100 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $λ_{1} = 7$ and $λ_{2} = 10$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is positive-definite\n3. $A$ is indefinite" 13194139535801 false] [17592186162563 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186162563 1145 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186162565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535801 true] [17592186162565 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186162565 1100 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} =-1$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 false] [17592186162565 1145 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366925 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366926 13194139535801 true] [17592186366925 1089 "q-139436" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366925 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535801 true] [17592186366925 1100 "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366925 1113 #uuid "a8ff653f-0db2-5e4b-99bc-705651d85f22" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366925 1145 "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366927 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366928 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366929 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366930 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366931 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1041 "selection" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366931 13194139535801 true] [17592186366926 1110 #uuid "6c6d3913-98b1-5983-9c30-56a99dba7e95" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366927 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366927 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366927 1111 #uuid "10289142-329b-5cb8-b39a-7a56c9ac66fa" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366928 1034 "I only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366928