gned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366962 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366963 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366964 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366965 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366966 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1041 "selection" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366963 13194139535801 true] [17592186366961 1110 #uuid "c5544bb8-4bac-525c-986f-d947aa317309" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366962 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366962 1111 #uuid "52c7f4f6-6077-546f-a7fd-db8a4f24420b" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366963 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366963 1111 #uuid "41171f9a-bd23-5bf9-b4fb-eb0bb4181f48" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366964 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366964 1111 #uuid "2638fec9-d27a-5a77-b823-343d8ea0c08d" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366965 1034 "III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366965 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366965 1111 #uuid "f70a9f8b-7811-5d36-894b-0818ed763318" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366966 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366966 1111 #uuid "76f3fde2-0b97-541a-b257-2c4422376b47" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366968 13194139535801 true] [17592186366967 1089 "q-94734" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535801 true] [17592186366967 1100 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{2}^{2}+7{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366967 1113 #uuid "8eb37d11-7d5f-52ca-ac94-2177d57f1e07" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366967 1145 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-6)^{2}+7\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =36+0 \\\\  & =36>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-72<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={0}^{2}-(-6)^{2}-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-36-0 \\\\  & =-36<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366969 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366970 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366971 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366972 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366973 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1041 "selection" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366970 13194139535801 true] [17592186366968 1110 #uuid "607cafa2-10fb-5b2d-8514-3267070a098d" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366969 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366969 1111 #uuid "a04fb800-ae92-5204-954d-621006726402" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366970 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366970 1111 #uuid "af91df7e-460b-5c9c-ac85-2dcda8fb703a" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366971 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366971 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366971 1111 #uuid "ac3715d0-9134-52d8-8a54-8abfbe3b9c98" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366972 1034 "III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366972 1111 #uuid "ef6573d8-cbc2-559c-98e3-2010f618983d" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366973 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366973 1111 #uuid "2b42d044-3e6b-5f93-9b21-885d9eec3750" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366975 13194139535801 true] [17592186366974 1089 "q-94744" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535801 true] [17592186366974 1100 "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366974 1113 #uuid "adc08558-14b4-5986-ac1e-110d6dbcb8a4" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366974 1145 "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366976 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366977 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366978 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366979 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366980 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1041 "selection" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366980 13194139535801 true] [17592186366975 1110 #uuid "96080315-dd72-5098-b4a0-7029a90b9712" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366976 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366976 1111 #uuid "0f813a63-ed9b-556c-90f4-ee76f89ffaeb" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366977 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366977 1111 #uuid "05a1f8cc-a779-54d8-8947-0cf2d637e366" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366978 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366978 1111 #uuid "2bd169a7-79c1-5b07-9f3a-b3319ece1659" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366979 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366979 1111 #uuid "46148325-6813-531c-a8ea-47eac22bde8a" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366980 1034 "II only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366980 1111 #uuid "2381036d-ee34-5f27-9d82-953f70ba7173" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366981 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366982 13194139535801 true] [17592186366981 1089 "q-94795" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366981 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535801 true] [17592186366981 1100 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366981 1113 #uuid "a9d123b6-e467-5363-8d7e-2f42b2dd2007" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366981 1145 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366983 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366984 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366985 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366986 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366987 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1041 "selection" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366986 13194139535801 true] [17592186366982 1110 #uuid "f3d899bf-6bf7-53fd-8054-85ec15073d7a" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366983 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366983 1111 #uuid "0310574b-4390-5ee9-97d7-3ef3e67d2c01" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366984 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535801 true] [17592186366984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535801 true] [17592186366984 1111 #uuid "6cd054b